From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SONiC VPP <sonic-vpp@sonic-net.github.io>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sflow: switch-wide drop monitoring with a usable IN_PORT

Make the drop monitor usable by SONiC: report the ingress port in a form a
NET_DM listener can parse and resolve, report it against the port the
packet actually arrived on, and monitor drops on every interface rather
than only on ports with packet sampling enabled.

IN_PORT encoding
----------------

send_discard_sample() writes NET_DM_ATTR_IN_PORT as a bare u32 holding the
VPP hw_if_index. Both halves of that are wrong for a NET_DM listener.

The kernel's drop_monitor encodes NET_DM_ATTR_IN_PORT as a nested container
whose NET_DM_ATTR_PORT_NETDEV_IFINDEX member carries the ifIndex, and that
is what listeners parse. hsflowd's mod_dropmon, for one, casts the payload
to a struct nlattr and walks it: a bare 4-byte u32 presents as nla_len=1,
which fails the "nla_len >= sizeof(struct nlattr)" check, so the walk never
executes its body and the ingress port is reported as 0.

The ifIndex itself is also in the wrong namespace. A NET_DM listener indexes
interfaces by Linux ifIndex, not by VPP hw_if_index. The sflow plugin already
resolves the pairing for the counter channel, where it dlsyms linux-cp's
lcp_itf_pair_get_vif_index_by_phy() and advertises the result as
SFLOW_VPP_ATTR_OSINDEX. Reuse that here so a discard on, say, VPP interface
bobm0 is reported against the ifIndex of its host tap, matching the ifIndex
the same listener already has counters and packet samples for. The pairing
is looked up from the hw interface rather than per_interface_data, since
discards are reported from interfaces sampling is not enabled on too.

Fall back to the hw_if_index when linux-cp is absent or the interface has no
pairing, which is no worse than the current behaviour.

Note the packet-sample path (SFLOWPS_PSAMPLE_ATTR_IIFINDEX) is deliberately
left alone: it is a plain u32 by PSAMPLE's own definition, and hsflowd's
mod_vpp translates it from the VPP namespace itself.

Original ingress port
---------------------

The RX sw_if_index a discard carries is often no longer the port the
packet arrived on: bond-input rewrites it to the bond, l2-input to a BVI,
ethernet-input to a sub-interface, and so on, so the drop would be reported
against a port-channel or VLAN rather than a physical port.  SONiC wants the
port the packet came in on.  The sonic_ext plugin's sonic-ext-capture node
records exactly that, on the device-input arc before any of those nodes run,
so dlsym sonic_ext's sonic_ext_buffer_orig_rx_sw_if_index() alongside the
linux-cp lookup and report the port it returns whenever it returns one.
That accessor only answers for a packet capture actually ran on - capture
flags the buffer, and the flag, unlike the value it guards, is cleared when
the buffer is recycled - so otherwise, or when sonic_ext is not loaded, the
current RX interface is reported as before.

Switch-wide drop monitoring
---------------------------

Drop monitoring is a switch-wide setting, but sflow-drop was only put on
the error-drop arc of interfaces that packet sampling had been enabled on.
SONiC has no per-port drop monitor: drop_monitor_limit lives in
SFLOW|global and orchagent binds its TAM object to the switch, and hsflowd's
mod_dropmon reports every discard it receives against one datasource.

Tying the two together also loses discards on ports where sampling *is*
enabled.  error-drop dispatches a frame on the RX sw_if_index its packets
carry, and that is the bond once bond-input has run, a sub-interface after
ethernet-input, or a BVI after l2-input - none of which SONiC enables
sampling on.  A discard on a port-channel member was therefore never seen,
whatever IN_PORT it would have been given.

Put sflow-drop on every interface while drop monitoring is on, and on any
interface created while it is; sflow enable/disable no longer touches it.
That also drops the per-interface toggle, which passed smp->dropM rather
than the desired state and so, on disabling sampling on a port with drop
monitoring on, enabled sflow-drop a second time instead of removing it.

Drop monitoring alone now starts and stops the sample-processing process
as well.  sflow_sampling_start_stop() already counted dropM as a reason to
run, but nothing called it when dropM changed, so with no port sampling
the discards sat in the worker FIFOs - and sflow-drop could be reached
before per_thread_data had been allocated.

Drop notification rate limit
----------------------------

SONiC caps drop monitoring at drop_monitor_limit notifications per second,
which orchagent expresses as a packet-mode policer (CIR = CBS = limit) on
the trap group its TAM collector traps to.  An ASIC meters the mirrored
drops with it; here nothing did, so a drop storm went to hsflowd at
whatever rate the 4-deep worker drop FIFOs and the 1 ms poll let through.

The SAI layer already turns every SAI policer into a VPP policer, so meter
the notifications with that one rather than with a limiter of our own: the
policer is then created, updated, removed and counted exactly like any
other, and only how it is attached differs.  The main-thread FIFO drain
runs each discard through vnet_police_packet() before send_discard_sample()
and drops it if the policer's action for the verdict is drop, the way the
sonic_ext copp-ip2me node meters punted packets.  A pps policer is metered
at the policer plugin's fixed 256-byte packet size, so it counts
notifications; a byte-rate one at the sampled packet size.  The verdicts
are added to the policer's own conform/exceed/violate counters.  Metering
on the main thread gives one exact switch-wide limit with no cross-thread
state, and nothing else polices through this policer, so it needs no lock.

vnet_police_packet() scales the packet length in 32 bits by the shift that
just fits the policer's bucket limits, so a length beyond every bucket
wraps.  For drop_monitor_limit = 1, CIR = CBS = 1 is a 250-byte bucket
(xlate.c rounds 1 pps to 2 kbps, and a pps burst is in ms of that rate),
and a 256-byte notification scaled by clz(250) = 24 is exactly 2^32: it
was metered as 0 bytes and every one conformed.  A length that cannot fit
a bucket can never conform or exceed, so it is now a violate without
calling vnet_police_packet(); the skipped refill is caught up from
last_update_time by the next call.

The policer is bound by name and resolved once per FIFO drain, at the first
drop dequeued, rather than per notification: policer add/update/delete and
the binding API run on the main thread too and a drain never suspends, so
the policer cannot change mid-drain and a change is seen by the next 1 ms
poll.  So it may be bound before it exists and replaced or deleted
afterwards; while it does not resolve the
notifications are unlimited, as they are with no name bound, the default.
The policer plugin is reached through vlib_get_plugin_symbol(), so sflow
does not need it loaded.  Suppressed discards are counted in a new "sflow
DROPMON rate-limited" node counter and in "show sflow".  Configured by the
sflow_drop_policer_set API, read back by sflow_drop_policer_get, and by
"sflow drop-monitoring policer <name>|none".

Tests
-----

Verifying the encoding needs the bytes that were sent, but a NET_DM message
is published to a netlink multicast group, so reading it back means
attaching a listener - which needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and a kernel built with
CONFIG_NET_DROP_MONITOR. Add "sflow dropmon-capture enable", which retains
the last message exactly as it was handed to sendmsg(), and "show sflow
dropmon-capture", which hexdumps it. That makes the encoding inspectable on
a live system without a listener, and lets SFlowDropMonInPortTestCase in
test_sflow_drop.py walk IN_PORT the way a listener would and assert it is a
nested container carrying the linux-cp ifIndex rather than the VPP index.

SFlowDropAllInterfacesTestCase enables drop monitoring with sampling off on
every interface and checks that sflow-drop is on each interface's arc,
including one created afterwards, that a discard is processed, and that
the feature is removed again when drop monitoring is turned off.  It also
replays the per-port toggle bug: with sampling on two ports and drop
monitoring on, disabling sampling on one must leave sflow-drop on its arc
exactly once, and turning drop monitoring off must then remove it from both
and stop that port's discards being processed while the other port is still
sampled.

SFlowDropPolicerTestCase binds a policer name before the policer exists
and checks nothing is limited, then creates a 1 pps policer with a 36 s
(9000-byte, 35-notification) burst and sends discards one at a time,
waiting for the main thread to dequeue each one ("show sflow" counts it as
sent, dropped or rate-limited) so the 3-entry worker FIFO never overflows
however often the drain runs, and a send that fails for want of a NET_DM
family still counts.  It checks that no more than the burst
plus rate * elapsed reach the sender, that the rest are counted as
rate-limited, that the policer's own stats account for every notification
at 256 bytes each, and that nothing is limited once the policer is unbound
or deleted.  A second test binds the 250-byte 1 pps, 1 s policer and
checks that every notification is dropped rather than wrapped to 0 bytes.

Signed-off-by: SONiC VPP <sonic-vpp@sonic-net.github.io>
---
diff --git a/src/plugins/sflow/node.c b/src/plugins/sflow/node.c
index 3f45c97e2..b59924d8c 100644
--- a/src/plugins/sflow/node.c
+++ b/src/plugins/sflow/node.c
@@ -391,6 +391,28 @@ buffer_rewind_current (vlib_buffer_t *bN)
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * The RX sw_if_index a discard carries is often no longer the port the packet
+ * arrived on: bond-input rewrites it to the bond, l2-input to a BVI, and so
+ * on.  Report the port recorded by sonic_ext's sonic-ext-capture whenever it
+ * is available, and the current RX interface otherwise.
+ */
+static_always_inline u32
+sflow_drop_rx_sw_if_index (sflow_main_t *smp, vlib_buffer_t *bN)
+{
+  u32 rx = vnet_buffer (bN)->sw_if_index[VLIB_RX];
+
+  if (smp->sonic_ext_buffer_orig_rx_sw_if_index == 0)
+    return rx;
+
+  u32 orig_rx = (*smp->sonic_ext_buffer_orig_rx_sw_if_index) (bN);
+  /* the port may have been deleted while the packet was in flight */
+  if (orig_rx == ~0 ||
+      !vnet_get_sw_interface_or_null (smp->vnet_main, orig_rx))
+    return rx;
+  return orig_rx;
+}
+
 VLIB_NODE_FN (sflow_drop_node)
 (vlib_main_t *vm, vlib_node_runtime_t *node, vlib_frame_t *frame)
 {
@@ -416,7 +438,7 @@ VLIB_NODE_FN (sflow_drop_node)
       ethernet_header_t *en = vlib_buffer_get_current (bN);
       // Where did this packet come in originally?
       // (Doesn't have to be known)
-      u32 if_index = vnet_buffer (bN)->sw_if_index[VLIB_RX];
+      u32 if_index = sflow_drop_rx_sw_if_index (smp, bN);
       if (if_index)
 	{
 	  vnet_hw_interface_t *hw =
diff --git a/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.api b/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.api
index 9b722bc20..45927f0ce 100644
--- a/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.api
+++ b/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.api
@@ -263,6 +263,50 @@ define sflow_drop_monitoring_get_reply
   option in_progress;
 };
 
+/** @brief API to meter sflow drop notifications through a VPP policer
+    @param client_index - opaque cookie to identify the sender
+    @param context - sender context, to match reply w/ request
+    @param name - policer name, empty = unlimited.  Resolved on use, so the
+                  policer need not exist yet.
+*/
+
+autoreply define sflow_drop_policer_set {
+    /* Client identifier, set from api_main.my_client_index */
+    u32 client_index;
+
+    /* Arbitrary context, so client can match reply to request */
+    u32 context;
+
+    string name[64];
+};
+
+/** @brief API to get the policer sflow drop notifications are metered by
+    @param client_index - opaque cookie to identify the sender
+    @param context - sender context, to match reply w/ request
+*/
+
+define sflow_drop_policer_get {
+    /* Client identifier, set from api_main.my_client_index */
+    u32 client_index;
+
+    /* Arbitrary context, so client can match reply to request */
+    u32 context;
+};
+
+/** \brief reply to get the policer sflow drop notifications are metered by
+    @param context - sender context, to match reply w/ request
+    @param name - policer name, empty = unlimited
+    @param rate_limited - drop notifications the policer dropped
+*/
+
+define sflow_drop_policer_get_reply
+{
+  u32 context;
+  string name[64];
+  u32 rate_limited;
+  option in_progress;
+};
+
 /** \brief Dump sflow enabled interface(s)
     @param client_index - opaque cookie to identify the sender
     @param hw_if_index - hw_if_index of a specific interface, or -1 (default)
diff --git a/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.c b/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.c
index d8e371d18..f12c78f5a 100644
--- a/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.c
+++ b/src/plugins/sflow/sflow.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <sflow/sflow.api_enum.h>
 #include <sflow/sflow.api_types.h>
 #include <sflow/sflow_dlapi.h>
+#include <policer/policer.h>
 
 #include <vpp-api/client/stat_client.h>
 #include <vlib/stats/stats.h>
@@ -349,6 +350,28 @@ send_packet_sample (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp, sflow_sample_t *sample)
   return 1;
 }
 
+/* Map a VPP hw_if_index onto the Linux ifIndex of the linux-cp tap interface
+   that shadows it.  That is the namespace hsflowd indexes its interfaces by,
+   and it is already what we advertise as SFLOW_VPP_ATTR_OSINDEX on the
+   counter channel.  Returns 0 if there is no linux-cp pairing. */
+static u32
+sflow_linux_if_index (sflow_main_t *smp, u32 hw_if_index)
+{
+  if (smp->lcp_itf_pair_get_vif_index_by_phy == 0)
+    return 0;
+  /* Discards are monitored on every interface, not just the ones sampling is
+     enabled on, so per_interface_data cannot be relied on to describe it. */
+  vnet_hw_interface_t *hw =
+    vnet_get_hw_interface_or_null (smp->vnet_main, hw_if_index);
+  if (!hw)
+    return 0;
+  u32 linux_if_index =
+    (*smp->lcp_itf_pair_get_vif_index_by_phy) (hw->sw_if_index);
+  if (linux_if_index == INDEX_INVALID)
+    return 0;
+  return linux_if_index;
+}
+
 static int
 send_discard_sample (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp,
 		     sflow_sample_t *sample)
@@ -399,7 +422,20 @@ send_discard_sample (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp,
   // TODO: read from header? (really just needs to be non-zero for hsflowd)
   u16 proto = 0x0800;
   SFLOWDM_set_attr_int (dmt, NET_DM_ATTR_PROTO, proto);
-  SFLOWDM_set_attr_int (dmt, NET_DM_ATTR_IN_PORT, sample->input_if_index);
+  /* NET_DM_ATTR_IN_PORT is a nested container, so encode it that way.
+     Report the Linux ifIndex of the paired linux-cp tap when we have one,
+     since the ifIndex has to make sense to the NET_DM listener; otherwise
+     fall back to the VPP hw_if_index. */
+  u32 in_port_if_index = sflow_linux_if_index (smp, sample->input_if_index);
+  if (in_port_if_index == 0)
+    in_port_if_index = sample->input_if_index;
+  SFLOWDMInPort in_port = {
+    .nla = { .nla_type = NET_DM_ATTR_PORT_NETDEV_IFINDEX,
+	     .nla_len = sizeof (struct nlattr) + sizeof (u32) },
+    .if_index = in_port_if_index
+  };
+  SFLOWDM_set_attr_nested (dmt, NET_DM_ATTR_IN_PORT, &in_port,
+			   sizeof (in_port));
   SFLOWDM_set_attr (dmt, NET_DM_ATTR_PAYLOAD, sample->header,
 		    sample->header_bytes);
   if (SFLOWDM_send (dmt) < 0)
@@ -407,6 +443,76 @@ send_discard_sample (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp,
   return 1;
 }
 
+/* the packet size the policer plugin's xlate.c converts a pps rate with, so
+   metering every notification at this size meters them in packets */
+#define SFLOW_DROP_POLICER_PPS_BYTES 256
+
+static policer_main_t *sflow_policer_main;
+static vlib_combined_counter_main_t *sflow_policer_counters;
+
+/* Resolved lazily by name, so the policer may be created, replaced or
+   deleted independently of the binding. */
+static policer_t *
+sflow_drop_policer_resolve (sflow_main_t *smp)
+{
+  if (vec_len (smp->drop_policer_name) == 0)
+    return 0;
+  if (!sflow_policer_main)
+    {
+      sflow_policer_main = policer_get_main ();
+      sflow_policer_counters = policer_get_counters ();
+      if (!sflow_policer_main)
+	return 0;
+    }
+
+  policer_main_t *pm = sflow_policer_main;
+  uword *p = hash_get_mem (pm->policer_index_by_name, smp->drop_policer_name);
+  if (!p || pool_is_free_index (pm->policers, p[0]))
+    {
+      smp->drop_policer_index = ~0;
+      return 0;
+    }
+  smp->drop_policer_index = p[0];
+
+  /* an in-place replace may change the rate type */
+  uword *cfg =
+    hash_get_mem (pm->policer_config_by_name, smp->drop_policer_name);
+  smp->drop_policer_pps =
+    cfg && pm->configs[cfg[0]].rate_type == QOS_RATE_PPS;
+
+  return pool_elt_at_index (pm->policers, smp->drop_policer_index);
+}
+
+/* Only this process node meters through the drop policer - it is on no
+   packet path - so vnet_police_packet() needs no lock here. */
+static bool
+sflow_drop_police (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp, policer_t *policer,
+		   sflow_sample_t *sample)
+{
+  if (!policer)
+    return true;
+
+  u32 len = smp->drop_policer_pps ? SFLOW_DROP_POLICER_PPS_BYTES :
+				    sample->sampled_packet_size;
+  policer_result_e verdict;
+  /* vnet_police_packet() scales the length in 32 bits by the shift that just
+     fits the bucket limits, so a length beyond every bucket wraps - a 256-byte
+     notification against a 1 pps, 1 s bucket of 250 bytes wraps to 0 - and
+     conforms.  It can never fit a bucket, so it violates; skipping the refill
+     loses nothing, since the next one catches up from last_update_time. */
+  if (((u64) len << policer->scale) >> 32)
+    verdict = POLICE_VIOLATE;
+  else
+    verdict =
+      vnet_police_packet (policer, len, POLICE_CONFORM,
+			  clib_cpu_time_now () >> POLICER_TICKS_PER_PERIOD_SHIFT);
+  if (sflow_policer_counters)
+    vlib_increment_combined_counter (&sflow_policer_counters[verdict],
+				     vm->thread_index,
+				     smp->drop_policer_index, 1, len);
+  return policer->action[verdict] != QOS_ACTION_DROP;
+}
+
 static u32
 read_worker_fifos (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp)
 {
@@ -434,12 +540,17 @@ read_worker_fifos (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp)
   // designed to be tolerant to random packet-loss in transit. For example, 1%
   // loss should just make it look like the sampling-rate setting was 1:10100
   // instead of 1:10000.
+  // The drop policer is resolved once per drain, at the first drop sample:
+  // policer add/update/delete and sflow_drop_policer_set() run on this
+  // thread too, and this drain never suspends, so it cannot change mid-drain.
+  policer_t *drop_policer = 0;
+  bool drop_policer_resolved = false;
   u32 batch = 0;
   for (; batch < SFLOW_READ_BATCH; batch++)
     {
       u32 psample_found = 0, dropmon_found = 0;
       u32 psample_send = 0, psample_send_fail = 0;
-      u32 dropmon_send = 0, dropmon_send_fail = 0;
+      u32 dropmon_send = 0, dropmon_send_fail = 0, dropmon_rate_limited = 0;
       for (clib_thread_index_t thread_index = 0;
 	   thread_index < smp->total_threads; thread_index++)
 	{
@@ -458,6 +569,16 @@ read_worker_fifos (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp)
 	  if (sflow_drop_fifo_dequeue (&sfwk->drop_fifo, &sample))
 	    {
 	      dropmon_found++;
+	      if (!drop_policer_resolved)
+		{
+		  drop_policer = sflow_drop_policer_resolve (smp);
+		  drop_policer_resolved = true;
+		}
+	      if (!sflow_drop_police (vm, smp, drop_policer, &sample))
+		{
+		  dropmon_rate_limited++;
+		  continue;
+		}
 	      int sent = send_discard_sample (vm, smp, &sample);
 	      if (sent == 1)
 		dropmon_send++;
@@ -500,6 +621,13 @@ read_worker_fifos (vlib_main_t *vm, sflow_main_t *smp)
 					   dropmon_send_fail);
 	      smp->dropmon_send_drops += dropmon_send_fail;
 	    }
+	  if (dropmon_rate_limited > 0)
+	    {
+	      vlib_node_increment_counter (smp->vlib_main, sflow_node.index,
+					   SFLOW_ERROR_DROPMON_RATE_LIMITED,
+					   dropmon_rate_limited);
+	      smp->dropmon_rate_limited += dropmon_rate_limited;
+	    }
 	}
     }
   return batch;
@@ -702,6 +830,7 @@ sflow_sampling_start (sflow_main_t *smp)
   smp->csample_send_drops = 0;
   smp->dropmon_send = 0;
   smp->dropmon_send_drops = 0;
+  smp->dropmon_rate_limited = 0;
 
   /* open PSAMPLE netlink channel for writing packet samples */
   SFLOWPS_init (&smp->sflow_psample);
@@ -801,14 +930,11 @@ sflow_enable_disable_interface (sflow_main_t *smp,
   bool egress_on =
     sfif->sflow_enabled &&
     (direction == SFLOW_DIRN_EGRESS || direction == SFLOW_DIRN_BOTH);
-  bool drop_on = sfif->sflow_enabled && smp->dropM;
   bool ingress_enabled = (vnet_feature_is_enabled ("device-input", "sflow",
 						   sfif->sw_if_index) == 1);
   bool egress_enabled =
     (vnet_feature_is_enabled ("interface-output", "sflow-egress",
 			      sfif->sw_if_index) == 1);
-  bool drop_enabled = (vnet_feature_is_enabled ("error-drop", "sflow-drop",
-						sfif->sw_if_index) == 1);
 
   if (ingress_on != ingress_enabled)
     vnet_feature_enable_disable ("device-input", "sflow", sfif->sw_if_index,
@@ -816,11 +942,50 @@ sflow_enable_disable_interface (sflow_main_t *smp,
   if (egress_on != egress_enabled)
     vnet_feature_enable_disable ("interface-output", "sflow-egress",
 				 sfif->sw_if_index, egress_on, 0, 0);
-  if (drop_on != drop_enabled)
-    vnet_feature_enable_disable ("error-drop", "sflow-drop", sfif->sw_if_index,
+}
+
+/*
+ * Drop monitoring is switch-wide, independent of which ports are sampled:
+ * SONiC binds its drop-monitor TAM object to the switch.  error-drop also
+ * dispatches a frame on the RX sw_if_index its packets carry, which may be a
+ * bond, a sub-interface or a BVI rather than a port sampling is enabled on, so
+ * sflow-drop has to be on every interface while drop monitoring is on.
+ */
+static void
+sflow_drop_enable_disable_interface (sflow_main_t *smp, u32 sw_if_index)
+{
+  bool drop_enabled = (vnet_feature_is_enabled ("error-drop", "sflow-drop",
+						sw_if_index) == 1);
+
+  /* vnet_feature_enable_disable() is not idempotent: enabling twice would
+     add sflow-drop to the arc twice and report every discard twice. */
+  if (smp->dropM != drop_enabled)
+    vnet_feature_enable_disable ("error-drop", "sflow-drop", sw_if_index,
 				 smp->dropM, 0, 0);
 }
 
+static void
+sflow_drop_enable_disable_all (sflow_main_t *smp)
+{
+  vnet_sw_interface_t *si;
+
+  pool_foreach (si, smp->vnet_main->interface_main.sw_interfaces)
+    sflow_drop_enable_disable_interface (smp, si->sw_if_index);
+}
+
+static clib_error_t *
+sflow_sw_interface_add_del (vnet_main_t *vnm, u32 sw_if_index, u32 is_add)
+{
+  /* Deletion strips every feature from the interface already.  Interfaces
+     created during early init may arrive before the feature arcs exist, but
